<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Laoshang Chanyu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 161 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Jiyu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Laoshang was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyu</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> from 174 to 161 BCE, the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modu-chanyu">Modu</a>. He completed the defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a>, killing their king and, according to Chinese sources, making his skull into a drinking cup, which drove the Yuezhi westwards. He kept up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heqin">heqin</a> arrangement with Han <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, receiving a Han princess, while raiding the frontier.</p></div>
